Scoped Limited Auditor — Team Test Setup
Example test objects

Use the following naming pattern:

AD group: CA_PAM_AUD_<TEAM>
Test auditor: One non-PAM administrative user
Team account Safe: Existing Safe containing the team’s accounts
Recording Safe: PSMREC_<TEAM>
Platform: The platform assigned to the team’s test account
1. Create the auditor AD group

Create an AD security group such as:

CA_PAM_AUD_LINUXTEAM

Add only the test auditor account to the group and confirm the group is synchronized into CyberArk Identity.

Keep the group in the normal Privilege Cloud Users role required for portal access, but do not add it to:

Privilege Cloud Auditors
Privilege Cloud Session Risk Managers
Privilege Cloud Safe Managers
Global Auditor
Privilege Cloud Administrators

The Privilege Cloud Auditor role provides broad access to audits, reports, and recordings, while Global Auditor provides access to the shared Audit service. Neither is appropriate for a team-scoped test.

2. Grant access to the team’s account Safe

Navigate to:

Privilege Cloud Portal
→ Administration
→ Safe Management
→ Select the team Safe
→ Members
→ Add member

Add:

CA_PAM_AUD_LINUXTEAM

Grant only:

Account Safe permission	Setting
List accounts/files	Yes
View audit	Yes
Use accounts	No
Retrieve accounts/files	No
Add accounts	No
Update account properties	No
Update account content	No
Initiate CPM operations	No
Specify next password	No
Delete accounts	No
Unlock accounts	No
Manage Safe	No
Manage Safe members	No
Confirm requests	No
Access Safe without confirmation	No

List accounts/files allows access to recording links from the Account Details page, while View audit provides activity visibility for that Safe. Do not grant Use accounts or Retrieve accounts/files, because those permissions could allow connections or secret retrieval.

3. Configure a team-specific recording Safe

Navigate to:

Administration
→ Platform Management
→ Locate the team platform
→ More options
→ Edit
→ Target Account Platform
→ UI & Workflows
→ Privileged Session Management

Configure:

SessionRecorderSafe = PSMREC_LINUXTEAM
SessionRecorderSafeRetention = 30

Save the platform.

CyberArk creates the recording Safe when the first recording for that platform is uploaded. Recordings created before this change remain in the previous recording Safe, normally PSMRecordings.

Important for a shared platform

Do not assign a single team recording Safe to a platform used by multiple unrelated teams. That would place all future sessions using that platform into the same recording Safe.

For a shared platform, use one of these approaches:

SessionRecorderSafe = PSM-{AccountSafeName}

This creates separate recording Safes based on the account Safe name.

Alternatively, clone the platform and assign the team’s accounts to the cloned platform. CyberArk supports static recording Safe names and dynamic names containing {AccountSafeName}.

4. Generate the first test recording

Using a normal privileged user—not the auditor:

Connect through PSM to an account assigned to the selected platform.
Perform a harmless test action.
End the session.
Confirm that PSMREC_LINUXTEAM appears under Safe Management.
Confirm that the recording appears under Monitoring.

The Monitoring menu may not appear for the auditor until at least one session recording exists.

5. Grant access to the recording Safe

Navigate to:

Administration
→ Safe Management
→ PSMREC_LINUXTEAM
→ Members
→ Add member

Add:

CA_PAM_AUD_LINUXTEAM

Grant only:

Recording Safe permission	Setting
Retrieve accounts/files	Yes
List accounts/files	Yes
View audit	Yes
Use accounts	No
Add accounts	No
Update account properties	No
Update account content	No
Delete accounts	No
Manage Safe	No
Manage Safe members	No

CyberArk requires Retrieve accounts/files, List accounts/files, and View audit on the recording Safe for granular recording access. The auditor does not need membership in the Privilege Cloud Auditor role.

6. Test the auditor account

Sign in using the Limited Auditor test account.

Navigate to:

Privilege Cloud Portal
→ Monitoring

Confirm the auditor can:

See the team’s recording.
Search by user, target address, connection type, or date.
View session start time and duration.
Open and play the recording.
Review the activity timeline.
View account activity for the authorized account Safe.

CyberArk’s Monitoring page supports searching recordings by session properties, activities, and date ranges.

7. Perform negative testing

Confirm the auditor cannot:

View recordings from another team’s recording Safe.
View accounts from another team’s account Safe.
Show or copy passwords.
Connect using the account.
Change or reconcile passwords.
Modify account properties.
Add or delete accounts.
Add or remove Safe members.
Modify platforms.
Terminate active sessions.
Access tenant-wide Audit and Reports information.

Also verify that the user does not inherit broader access from another nested AD group.

8. Test non-session audit visibility

For this scoped model, use the team account Safe’s View audit permission.

The auditor should be able to review activity associated with accounts in that Safe, such as:

Account usage
PSM connections
Password management activity
Account changes
User and timestamp information

Do not assign Global Auditor for this test. Global Auditor provides access to the shared Audit space, including activity searches, reports, and SIEM integration capabilities, and is not Safe-scoped.

Final permission model
AD Group:
CA_PAM_AUD_LINUXTEAM

Account Safe:
List accounts/files = Yes
View audit = Yes

Recording Safe:
Retrieve accounts/files = Yes
List accounts/files = Yes
View audit = Yes

Excluded roles:
Privilege Cloud Auditors
Global Auditor
Session Risk Managers
Safe Managers
Administrators

This gives the selected team access to its PSM recordings and Safe-level activity without providing password retrieval, connection, account management, platform management, or tenant-wide audit access.
